    16	# Design analysis for the fused variant
    17	# -------------------------------------
    18	# a) Concrete visual change and behavior trace:
    19	#    - The destination remains a solid n×n square.
    20	#    - The mover is no longer identified by color order. It is the unique n×n square
    21	#      body with one extra same-colored tip pixel on one side, forming a directional cue.
    22	#    - A single red control pixel is placed on one non-corner boundary cell. Its edge
    23	#      position encodes the forced first step: top->down, bottom->up, left->right,
    24	#      right->left.
    25	#    - The start region is generated with exactly two exits: the original corridor exit
    26	#      and one short dead-end stub. The tip points to one of these exits, while the red
    27	#      control pixel selects the actual first step. When they disagree, the control pixel
    28	#      overrides the tip. The tip therefore still matters visually, because some samples
    29	#      show agreement and others show conflict.
    30	#    - Small trace:
    31	#      original: sort colors -> pick lower-color square -> BFS from start over all exits.
    32	#      combo: detect arrow-shaped mover -> read its tip direction -> read border control
    33	#      direction -> force the first step to the control direction and forbid returning to
    34	#      the start -> slide only the square body; erase the input-only tip back to river.
    35	# b) Complexity calibration:
    36	#    - Only two extra cues are added: one protrusion pixel and one edge control pixel.
    37	#    - n is restricted to 2..4. This keeps the arrow decomposition legible and avoids
    38	#      confusion between 1×1 objects and 1×1 control pixels.
    39	#    - The generator enforces exactly two exits from the start after adding the stub, so
    40	#      "agreement vs conflict" is easy to see and does not depend on hidden tie-breaking.
    41	# c) Hidden assumptions broken from the original main():
    42	#    - "Both special objects are solid squares" fails because the mover has one tip.
    43	#    - "Lower color moves" fails because mover/destination colors are randomized.
    44	#    - "All reachable branches from the start are fair game" fails because the border
    45	#      control pixel forces a specific first step and the start cannot be revisited.
    46	#    - "Only the destination uses the red color" fails because the red control pixel is
    47	#      separate from the destination. The solver must detect the destination by shape, not
    48	#      by red color.
    49	# d) Placement legality:
    50	#    - The tip is painted onto an already-river cell adjacent to the mover body, so it
    51	#      never changes the true traversable geometry.
    52	#    - The control pixel is always on a non-corner boundary background cell and is kept
    53	#      4-disconnected from the destination square.
    54	#    - The stub is carved only through background cells and must leave the start with
    55	#      exactly two feasible exits.
    56	# e) Visual observability:
    57	#    - Across examples, humans can see one arrowed object, one plain target square, one
    58	#      red boundary dot, and outputs that sometimes follow the arrow and sometimes defy it.
    59	#      This makes the priority rule inferable from a few examples without color arithmetic.
    60	# f) Cleanliness:
    61	#    - The extra cues are sparse and non-overlapping: one tip attached to the mover and
    62	#      one isolated boundary pixel.
    63	#    - The generator rejects layouts where extra branches or overlaps would muddy the two
    64	#      relevant exit choices.
    65	# g) New solver assumptions introduced here and guaranteed by the generator:
    66	#    - Exactly one non-control component is a valid "square + one side tip" mover.
    67	#    - Exactly one other non-control component is a solid square of the same side length.
    68	#    - After removing the red control pixel, the mover start has exactly two feasible
    69	#      neighboring placements: the tip-indicated exit and the alternate exit.
    70	#    - The control direction is always one of those exits, and the tip direction is the
    71	#      other or the same exit when the cues agree.
    72	
